## How the Prompt Cache / Slot Reuse Works

The server's prompt cache mechanism has **two layers**:

### 1. Slot-level LCP similarity selection (`get_available_slot`)

In `server-context.cpp:1586–1695`, when a new task arrives, `get_available_slot()` tries to find a slot whose cached prompt shares a **longest common prefix (LCP)** with the incoming request's tokens:

```cpp
// fraction of the Longest Common Prefix length with respect to the input prompt length
const float sim_cur = float(tokens.get_common_prefix(task.tokens)) / task.tokens.size();

// select the current slot if the criteria match
if (sim_cur > sim_best && sim_cur > slot_prompt_similarity) {
    sim_best = sim_cur;
    ret = &slot;
}
```

If the selected slot would lose more than 50% of its existing context (`f_keep < 0.5f`), the slot's current prompt is **saved into the global prompt cache** before being overwritten:

```cpp
if (f_keep < 0.5f) {
    update_cache = true;
}
...
if (update_cache) {
    ret->prompt_save(*prompt_cache);
    if (!ret->prompt_load(*prompt_cache, task.tokens)) {
        ret->prompt_clear(false);
    }
    prompt_cache->update();
}
```

### 2. Global prompt cache lookup (`server_prompt_cache::load`)

In `server-task.cpp:1678–1751`, the cache searches all stored prompts for the one that **preserves the most context** while being most similar to the new request:

```cpp
bool server_prompt_cache::load(server_prompt & prompt, const server_tokens & tokens_new,
                               llama_context * ctx_tgt, llama_context * ctx_dft, int32_t id_slot) {
    const int lcp_best = prompt.tokens.get_common_prefix(tokens_new);

    float f_keep_best = prompt.tokens.size() > 0 ? float(lcp_best) / prompt.tokens.size() : -1.0f;
    float sim_best    = float(lcp_best) / tokens_new.size();

    auto it_best = states.end();

    // find the most similar cached prompt, that would also preserve the most context
    for (auto it = states.begin(); it != states.end(); ++it) {
        const int lcp_cur = it->tokens.get_common_prefix(tokens_new);

        const float f_keep_cur = float(lcp_cur) / it->tokens.size();
        const float sim_cur    = float(lcp_cur) / tokens_new.size();

        // don't trash large prompts
        if (f_keep_cur < 0.25f) {
            continue;
        }

        if (f_keep_best < f_keep_cur && sim_best < sim_cur) {
            f_keep_best = f_keep_cur;
            sim_best    = sim_cur;
            it_best = it;
        }
    }

    if (it_best != states.end()) {
        // restore KV state from the cached prompt
        llama_state_seq_set_data_ext(ctx_tgt, data.data(), size, id_slot, 0);
        prompt = std::move(*it_best);
        states.erase(it_best);
    }

    return true;
}
```

### 3. The actual common-prefix computation

In `server-common.cpp:471–516`, `get_common_prefix` walks token-by-token comparing two `server_tokens` objects:

```cpp
size_t server_tokens::get_common_prefix(const server_tokens & b) const {
    const size_t max_idx = std::min(tokens.size(), b.tokens.size());

    if (!has_mtmd) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < max_idx; ++i) {
            if (tokens[i] == b.tokens[i]) {
                continue;
            }
            return i;
        }
        return max_idx;
    }

    // multimodal-aware: LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL positions are matched by media chunk ID
    for (size_t i = 0; i < max_idx; ++i) {
        const llama_token ai = tokens[i];
        const llama_token bi = b.tokens[i];

        if (ai == LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL && bi == LLAMA_TOKEN_NULL) {
            const auto & a_chunk = find_chunk(i);
            const auto & b_chunk = b.find_chunk(i);
            const std::string id_ai = mtmd_input_chunk_get_id(a_chunk.get());
            const std::string id_bi = mtmd_input_chunk_get_id(b_chunk.get());
            const size_t n_tok_a = mtmd_input_chunk_get_n_tokens(a_chunk.get());
            const size_t n_tok_b = mtmd_input_chunk_get_n_tokens(b_chunk.get());

            if (id_ai == id_bi && n_tok_a == n_tok_b) {
                i += n_tok_a - 1; // skip the whole media chunk
                continue;
            }
            return i;
        }

        if (ai == bi) continue;
        return i;
    }
    return max_idx;
}
```

### 4. Cache eviction (`update`)

In `server-task.cpp:1753–1793`, the cache is pruned by size (MiB) and token-count limits, always keeping at least one entry:

```cpp
void server_prompt_cache::update() {
    if (limit_size > 0) {
        while (states.size() > 1 && size() > limit_size) {
            states.pop_front();  // remove oldest
        }
    }
    // ... token limit pruning similarly ...
}
```

### Summary of the flow

1. **`get_available_slot`** picks a slot either by LCP similarity (`slot_prompt_similarity` threshold) or LRU.
2. If the chosen slot's existing prompt would be largely discarded (`f_keep < 0.5`), it's **saved to the global cache** via `prompt_save` → `alloc` (which also deduplicates: skips if already cached, removes obsolete sub-prompts).
3. The new request's tokens are then matched against cached prompts via `prompt_load` → `load`, which finds the entry with the best combination of **context preservation** (`f_keep`) and **similarity** (`sim`), restores its KV state, and replaces the slot's prompt.
4. `update()` evicts old entries when the cache exceeds its configured RAM/token limits.

